The final [[heavy syllable]] of a root is [[Stress (linguistics)|stressed]].<ref name="Thelwall 1990 38"/>

The short vowels {{IPA|[u, ʊ, o, o̞, ɔ]}} are all possible [[allophone]]s of {{IPA|/u/}} across different dialects; e.g., {{lang|ar|قُلْت}} {{IPA|/ˈqult/}} ('I said') is pronounced {{IPA|[ˈqʊlt]}} or {{IPA|[ˈqolt]}} or {{IPA|[ˈqɔlt]}}, since the difference between the short [[mid vowels]] {{IPA|[o, o̞, ɔ]}} and {{IPA|[u, ʊ]}} is never phonemic, and they are mostly found in [[complementary distribution]], except for a number of speakers where they can be phonemic but only in foreign words.

The short vowels {{IPA|[i, ɪ, e, e̞, ɛ]}} are all possible allophones of {{IPA|/i/}} across different dialects; e.g., {{lang|ar|مِن}} {{IPA|/ˈmin/}} ('from') is pronounced {{IPA|[ˈmɪn]}} or {{IPA|[ˈmen]}} or {{IPA|[ˈmɛn]}} since the difference between the short mid vowels {{IPA|[e, e̞, ɛ]}} and {{IPA|[i, ɪ]}} is never phonemic, and they are mostly found in complementary distribution, except for a number of speakers where they can be phonemic but only in foreign words.

The long mid vowels {{IPA|/oː/}} and {{IPA|/eː/}} appear to be phonemic in most varieties of Arabic except in general [[Maghrebi Arabic]], where they merge with {{IPA|/uː/}} and {{IPA|/iː/}}. For example, لون ('color') is generally pronounced {{IPA|/loːn/}} in [[Mashriqi Arabic|Mashriqi dialects]] but {{IPA|/luːn/}} in most [[Maghrebi Arabic]]. The long mid vowels can be used in Modern Standard Arabic in dialectal words or in some stable loanwords or foreign names,<ref name="EMSA1">Elementary Modern Standard Arabic: Volume 1, by Peter F. Abboud (Editor), Ernest N. McCarus (Editor)</ref> as in {{lang|ar|روما}} {{IPA|/ˈroːma/}} ('Rome') and {{lang|ar|شيك}} {{IPA|/ˈʃeːk/}} ('cheque').

Foreign words often have a liberal sprinkling of long vowels, as vowels tend to be written as long vowels in foreign loans, under the influence of European-language orthographies which write down every vowel with a letter.<ref name="TYA">Teach Yourself Arabic, by Jack Smart (Author), Frances Altorfer (Author)</ref> The long mid vowels {{IPA|/eː/}} and {{IPA|/oː/}} are always rendered with the letters {{lang|ar|ي}} and {{lang|ar|و}}, respectively, accompanied by a preceding hamzah sitting above (أ) and below (إ) an alif (ا) respectively word-initially. In general, the pronunciation of loanwords is highly dependent on the speaker's native variety.

==Consonants==
{{See also|Arabic alphabet|sun and moon letters}}

Even in the most formal contexts, pronunciation of Arabic depends on the speaker's background,<ref>{{Harvcoltxt|Holes|2004|p=58}}</ref> even if the number and phonetic character of most of the 28 consonants has a broad degree of regularity among Arabic-speaking regions. Arabic is particularly rich in [[uvular consonant|uvular]], [[pharyngeal consonant|pharyngeal]], and [[pharyngealization|pharyngealized]] ("[[emphatic consonant|emphatic]]") sounds. 

