Long ([[geminate]] or double) consonants are pronounced exactly like short consonants, but last longer. In Arabic, they are called ''mushaddadah'' ("strengthened", marked with a [[shaddah]]). Between a long consonant and a pause, an [[epenthetic]] {{IPA|[ə]}} occurs,<ref name="Thelwall 1990 38"/> but this is only common across regions in West Asia.

==Phonotactics==
Standard Arabic syllables come in only five forms:<ref name="Ryding2005">{{Cite book |last=Ryding |first=Karin C. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=9J1e1iDF3YkC |title=A Reference Grammar of Modern Standard Arabic |date=2005-08-25 |publisher=Cambridge University Press |isbn=978-0-521-77151-1 |language=en}}</ref>
* CV (light)
* CVV (heavy)
* CVC (heavy)
* CVVC (super-heavy)
* CVCC (super-heavy)

Standard Arabic [[Syllable#Structure|syllable structure]] does not allow syllables to start with a vowel or with a consonant cluster.<ref name="Ryding2005" /> In cases where a word starts with a consonant cluster it is preceded by an epenthetic {{IPA|/ʔi/}} utterance initially or {{IPAslink|i}} when preceded by a word that ends with a consonant; there are however exceptions like {{lang|ar|من}} {{IPA|/min/|}} and {{lang|ar|ـهم}} {{IPA|/-hum/|}} that connect with a following word-initial consonant cluster with {{IPAslink|a}} and {{IPAslink|u}} respectively, if the preceding word ends with a long vowel that vowel is then shortened.

Super-heavy syllables are usually not allowed except word finally,<ref name="Ryding2005" /> with the exception of CVV- before geminates creating non-final CVVC- syllables, these can be found in the active participles of geminate Form I verbs, like in {{wikt-lang|ar|مادة}} {{IPA|/maːd.da/}} ('substance, matter'), {{wikt-lang|ar|كافة}} {{IPA|/kaːf.fa/}} ('entirely'). In the [[pausal form]], the final [[gemination|geminates]] behave as a single consonant, only when preceding another word or with [[Arabic diacritics|vocalization]], the geminates start appearing, belonging to two separate syllables. E.g.: {{wikt-lang|ar|سام}} {{IPA|/saːm(.m)/}} ('poisonous'), {{wikt-lang|ar|جاف}} {{IPA|/d͡ʒaːf(.f)/}} ('dry'), {{wikt-lang|ar|عام}} {{IPA|/ʕaːm(.m)/}} ('public, general'), {{wikt-lang|ar|خاص}} {{IPA|/χaːsˤ(.sˤ)/}} ('private, special'), and {{wikt-lang|ar|حار}} {{IPA|/ħaːr(.r)/}} ('hot, spicy').<ref name="Ryding2005" />

Loanwords can break some phonotactic rules like allowing initial consonant clusters (with an initial epenthetic {{IPAslink|i}} or often another repeated vowel from the word being optionally inserted after the first consonant) like in {{lang|ar|استاد}} {{IPA|/(i)s.taːd/}} "stadium" and {{lang|ar|فلورنسا}} {{IPA|/f(i).loː.ran.saː/}} "Florence" or allowing CVVC syllables non-finally without geminates like in {{lang|ar|روسيا}} {{IPA|/ruːs.jaː/}} "Russia" and {{lang|ar|سوريا}} {{IPA|/suːr.jaː/}} "Syria", which can be modified to {{IPA|/ruː.si.jaː, suː.ri.jaː/}} to fit the phonotactics better.<ref name="Ryding2005" />

==Word stress==
{{See also|Classical Arabic phonology}}
Stress in Modern Standard Arabic is generally consistent and standardized across the Arab world. Exceptions exist only in a limited set of scenarios, detailed below. Broadly speaking, stress is most likely to fall on the second-to-last syllable (the penultimate syllable), but frequently occurs in the final (ultimate) and third-to-last (antepenultimate) as well.

Arabic syllables can be categorized as light, heavy, and superheavy. This refers to the arrangement of vowels and consonants within the syllable.

With "C" representing a consonant, "V" representing a vowel, and "VV" representing a long vowel:

* Light:
** A syllable containing a short vowel (i.e. CV), such as وَ /wa/ ('and').
* Heavy:
** A syllable containing a long vowel (i.e. CVV), such as لَا /laː/ ('no').
** A syllable containing a short vowel followed by one consonant (i.e. CVC), such as مِن /min/ ('from').
* Super-heavy:
** A syllable containing a long vowel followed by one consonant (i.e. CVVC), such as بَاب /baːb/ ('door').
** A syllable containing a short vowel followed by two consonants (i.e. CVCC), such as بِنْت /bint/ ('girl'), or a long vowel followed by a geminate consonant (i.e. CVVC<sub>i</sub>C<sub>i</sub>), such as مادّ /maːdd/ ('stretching').

[[Standard Arabic phonology#Phonotactics|Phonotactic constraints in Arabic]] ensure that syllables do not generally fall outside of these patterns.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Broselow |first=Ellen |url=http://archive.org/details/the-encyclopedia-of-arabic-language-and-linguistics |title=The Encyclopedia of Arabic Language And Linguistics |date=2006-08-01 |volume=3 |pages=615–616}}</ref>
[[File:Arabic Stress Diagram.svg|alt=A flowchart depiction of Modern Standard Arabic stress placement.|thumb|A flowchart depiction of Modern Standard Arabic stress placement.|class=skin-invert-image]]
The following description of Arabic stress patterns is adapted from the description provided by Halpern (2009).<ref>{{Cite web |last=Halpern |first=Jack |date=January 2009 |title=Word Stress and Vowel Neutralization in Modern Standard Arabic |url=https://www.cjki.org/arabic/an_paper.pdf |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240424160637/https://www.cjki.org/arabic/an_paper.pdf |archive-date=2024-04-24 |website=The CJK Dictionary Institute}}</ref>

# Disregard all attached (prefixed) prepositions.<ref group="Note">A notable exception are words using the preposition لِ or بِ with a possessive enclitic, such as لَك and لَهُ, or بِنا and بِكم. These words are stressed on the first syllable even though this syllable is an attached prefix.</ref><ref group="Note">Note that this rule only excludes attached prefixes, and not possessive enclitics. Possessive suffixes like كَ- and هُم- will affect the stress.</ref> If this leaves only one syllable, stress it.
# If the final syllable is superheavy, stress it.
# Otherwise, if the word has only two syllables, stress the first one.
# Otherwise, if the second-to-last syllable is heavy, stress it.

When the above rules are insufficient to determine stress, speakers will employ different approaches to determine stress. These choices are influenced by the speaker's native dialect. Nearly all arrangements are possible. At least one source records four different stress placements for كتبتا /ka.ta.ba.taa/ ('two women wrote'):  /ˈka.ta.ba.taa/ (Upper Egypt), /ka.ˈta.ba.taa/ (Jordan), /ka.ta.ˈba.taa/ (Cairo), and /ka.ta.ba.ˈtaa/ (Lebanon).<ref>{{Cite book |last=Broselow |first=Ellen |url=http://archive.org/details/the-encyclopedia-of-arabic-language-and-linguistics |title=The Encyclopedia of Arabic Language And Linguistics |date=2006-08-01 |volume=3 |page=613}}</ref>

==Local variations of Modern Standard Arabic==

[[varieties of Arabic|Spoken varieties]] differ from [[Classical Arabic]] and [[Modern Standard Arabic]] not only in grammar but also in pronunciation. this variation might affect the way Modern Standard Arabic is spoken in each country or region.

Some examples of variation:

=== Consonants ===
The standard pronunciation of ⟨[[ج]]⟩ in MSA varies regionally, most prominently {{IPAblink|d͡ʒ}} in the [[Arabian Peninsula]], parts of the [[Levant]], [[Iraq]], north-central [[Algeria]], and southern [[Egypt]], it is also considered as the predominant pronunciation of Literary Arabic outside the Arab world and the pronunciation mostly used in Arabic loanwords across other languages (e.g. in Georgian, Malay, Persian, Turkish and Urdu), and {{IPAblink|ʒ}} in [[Morocco]], [[Tunisia]], [[Libya]], southern [[Algeria]], most of the Levant, eastern Arabian Peninsula. Other pronunciations include {{IPAblink|ɡ}} in Egypt, in coastal [[Yemen]], and [[Oman]], as well as {{IPAblink|ɟ}} in [[Sudan]] and hinterland Yemen.

In Modern Standard Arabic (not when it's native), {{IPA|/ɡ/}} is used as a marginal phoneme to pronounce some dialectal and loan words. On the other hand, it is considered a native [[phoneme]] or [[allophone]] in most modern Arabic dialects, mostly as a variant of {{lang|ar|ق}} {{IPA|/q/}} (as in [[Arab states of the Persian Gulf|Arabian Peninsula]] and [[Northwest African]] dialects) or as a variant of {{IPA|/d͡ʒ/}} {{lang|ar|ج}} (as in Egyptian and [[Sanʽani Arabic|Sanaani]] and Omani dialects). It is also considered a separate foreign phoneme that appears only in loanwords, as in most urban [[Levant]]ine dialects where {{lang|ar|ق}} is {{IPA|/ʔ/}} and {{lang|ar|ج}} is {{IPA|/d͡ʒ~ʒ/}}.

The dental {{lang|ar|ض}} {{IPA|[dˤ]}} was historically {{IPA|[ɮˤ]}}, a value it retains among older speakers in a few isolated dialects.<ref>Al-Azraqi. (2019). Delateralisation in Arabic and Mehri. ''Dialectologia'', 23: 1–23. https://raco.cat/index.php/Dialectologia/article/download/366597/460520/</ref>

'''Mergers and mispronunciations'''

Regional modern dialects may influence the way Modern Standard Arabic is spoken, which sometimes causes mergers or mispronunciations in consonants:

* Speakers that merge {{lang|ar|ض}} {{IPA|/dˤ/}} and {{lang|ar|ظ}} {{IPA|/ðˤ/}} to {{lang|ar|ظ}} {{IPA|/ðˤ/}} in their respective dialects usually mispronounce {{lang|ar|ض}} as {{lang|ar|ظ}} {{IPA|[ðˤ]}} when speaking Modern Standard Arabic instead of the standard {{IPA|[dˤ]}},<ref>{{Cite book |last=Hamdan |first=Jihad |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344811180 |title=The Arabic /dˤ/ Revisited: A Critical Review |publisher=Zarqa University |year=2020 |page=29}}</ref> e.g. {{lang|ar|ضار}} 'harmful' is pronounced {{IPA|[ðˤaːr]|}} instead of {{IPA|/dˤaːr/|}}.
* The voiced emphatic dental fricative {{lang|ar|ظ}} {{IPA|/ðˤ/}} is sometimes mispronounced as a voiced emphatic alveolar fricative {{IPA|[zˤ]}} depending on the speaker in Egypt, Sudan and Lebanon,<ref name="Watson 2002 19">{{Harvcoltxt|Watson|2002|p=19}}</ref> e.g. {{lang|ar|محافظة}} 'governorate' is pronounced {{IPA|[mu.ħaː.fa.zˤah]|}} instead of {{IPA|/mu.ħaː.fa.ðˤah/|}}.
* Speakers that lack the interdentals {{lang|ar|ث}} {{IPA|/θ/}} and {{lang|ar|ذ}} {{IPA|/ð/}} in their respective dialects, may merge them with {{lang|ar|ت}} {{IPAblink|t}} and {{lang|ar|د}} {{IPAblink|d}} or {{lang|ar|س}} {{IPAblink|s}} and {{lang|ar|ز}} {{IPAblink|z}}, respectively.
* Some speakers especially in Lebanon and Egypt might pronounce the standard uvular {{lang|ar|ق}} {{IPA|/q/}} as a plain velar {{lang|ar|ك}} {{IPAblink|k}}.
* A number of speakers in Yemen and among Bedu pronounce the uvular {{lang|ar|ق}} {{IPA|/q/}} as a velar {{IPAblink|g}} when speaking Modern Standard Arabic, e.g. {{lang|ar|لقد قلت لهم}} 'I told them' is pronounced {{IPA|/la.gad gul.tu la.hum/|}} instead of {{IPA|/la.qad qul.tu la.hum/|}}.
'''Foreign phonemes'''

The foreign phonemes {{IPA|/p/}}, {{IPA|/v/}}, {{IPA|/t͡ʃ/}}, {{IPA|/t͡s/}} etc. are not necessarily pronounced by all Arabic speakers, but they can be pronounced by some speakers especially in foreign proper nouns and loanwords. {{IPA|/p/}} and {{IPA|/v/}} are usually transcribed with their own letters {{lang|fa|ﭖ}} {{IPA|/p/}} and {{lang|ar|ﭪ}} {{IPA|/v/}} but as these letters are not part of Standard Arabic, and they are simply written with {{lang|ar|ب}} {{IPA|/b/}} and {{lang|ar|ف}} {{IPA|/f/}}, e.g. The use of both sounds may be considered marginal and Arabs may pronounce the words interchangeably; both {{lang|ar|نوفمبر}} and {{lang|ar|نوڤمبر}} {{IPA|/nu(ː)fambar/}}, {{IPA|/novambar, -ber/}} or {{IPA|/nofember/}} "November", both {{lang|ar|كاپريس}} and {{lang|ar|كابريس}} {{IPA|/ka(ː)pri(ː)s, ka(ː)bri(ː)s/}} "caprice" can be used.<ref name="TYA" /><ref name="Wehr">Hans Wehr, ''[[Dictionary of Modern Written Arabic]]'' (transl. of ''Arabisches Wörterbuch für die Schriftsprache der Gegenwart'', 1952)</ref>

{{IPA|/t͡ʃ/}} is a possible loanword phoneme, as in the word {{script/Arabic|سندوتش}} or {{script/Arabic|ساندوتش}} (''sandawitš'' or ''sāndwitš'' 'sandwich'), though a number of varieties instead break up the {{IPA|[t]}} and {{IPA|[ʃ]}} sounds with an epenthetic vowel.<ref>{{Harvcoltxt|Watson|2002|pp=60–62}}, citing Ṣan'ā'ni and Cairene as examples with and without this phoneme, respectively.</ref> Egyptian Arabic treats {{IPA|/t͡ʃ/}} as two consonants ({{IPA|[tʃ]}}) and inserts {{IPA|[e]}}, as [{{IPA|teʃ}}C] or [C{{IPA|etʃ}}], when it occurs before or after another consonant. {{IPA|/t͡ʃ/}} is found as normal in [[Iraqi Arabic]] and [[Gulf Arabic]] dialects.<ref name="tsh">{{Cite web|url=https://www.gulfarabic.com/p11_learn_arabic_sounds.php|title=The Arabic Sounds I. &#124; Gulf Arabic E-learning System|website=www.gulfarabic.com}}</ref> Normally the combination <big>{{lang|ar|تش}}</big> (''tā'-shīn'') is used to transliterate the {{IPA|[tʃ]}}. e.g. {{lang|ar|تشاد}} {{IPA|/t͡ʃaːd/}} "Chad".

=== Vowels ===
* Development of highly distinctive allophones of {{IPA|/a/}} and {{IPA|/aː/}}, with highly fronted {{IPA|[a(ː)]}}, {{IPA|[æ(ː)]}} or {{IPA|[ɛ(ː)]}} in non-emphatic contexts, and retracted {{IPA|[ɑ(ː)]}} in emphatic contexts.{{citation needed|date=April 2011}} The more extreme distinctions are characteristic of sedentary varieties, while Bedouin and conservative Arabian-peninsula varieties have much closer allophones. In some of the sedentary varieties, the allophones are gradually splitting into new phonemes under the influence of loanwords, where the allophone closest in sound to the source-language vowel often appears regardless of the presence or absence of nearby emphatic consonants.{{citation needed|date=April 2011}}
* Spread of "emphasis", visible in the backing of phonemic {{IPA|/a(ː)/}}. In conservative varieties of the Arabic peninsula, only {{IPA|/a/}} adjacent to emphatic consonants is affected, while in [[Cairo]], an emphatic consonant anywhere in a word tends to trigger emphatic allophones throughout the entire word.{{citation needed|date=April 2011}} Dialects of the Levant are somewhere in between. [[Moroccan Arabic]] is unusual in that {{IPA|/i/}} and {{IPA|/u/}} have clear emphatic allophones as well (typically lowered, e.g. to {{IPA|[e]}} and {{IPA|[o]}}).{{citation needed|date=April 2011}}
* The diphthongs {{IPA|/aj/}} and {{IPA|/aw/}} have monophthongized into {{IPA|/eː/}} and {{IPA|/oː/}} in most the Mashreqi dialects, these mid vowels may also be present in loanwords when speaking MSA, such as {{lang|ar|ملبورن}} ({{IPA|/mil.boːrn/}} [[Melbourne]]), {{lang|ar|سكرتير}} ({{IPA|/sikriteːr/}} '(male) secretary'),  {{lang|ar|روما}} ({{IPA|/roːmaː/}} [[Rome]]) and {{lang|ar|دكتور}} ({{IPA|/duktoːr/}} 'doctor').<ref name="EMSA1"/>
* Loss of final short vowels (with {{IPA|/i/}} sometimes remaining), and shortening of final long vowels. This triggered the loss of most Classical Arabic case and mood distinctions.{{citation needed|date=April 2011}}
* Change of [[Arabic grammar#Nisba|nisba]] suffix ''{{transliteration|ar|-iyy}}'' > ''{{transliteration|ar|-i}}'', the nisba suffix ''{{transliteration|ar|-iyy}}'' as {{lang|ar|عَرَبِيّ}} ({{IPA|/ʕarabijj/}} '(male) Arabic') is usually mispronounced ''{{transliteration|ar|-i}}'' {{IPA|/ʕarabi/}} by many speakers.
* Shorten of final long ''{{transliteration|ar|-ī}}'' > ''{{transliteration|ar|-i}}''  {{lang|ar|حُبِّي}} ({{IPA|/ħubbiː/}} 'my love') is usually pronounced {{IPA|/ħubbi/}} with a short final ''{{transliteration|ar|-i}}'' by many speakers.

